{"about":"6bdc16fc61c220036acbccfe16be6e8f7747318e53c86d376b81b4161576af73","body":"The conjecture conflates 'preference for action' with 'belief in future success.' Popper explicitly distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which remains unproven) and the pragmatic decision to adopt it. Preferring the better-corroborated theory is a rational choice based on the available critical discussion, not an inductive inference that the theory is probable or will remain true. One can choose the 'best tested' map for navigation without assuming the territory will not change, thereby avoiding the smuggled induction the conjecture alleges.\n[would settle: A clear textual analysis of Popper's distinction between 'corroboration' as a report of past performance versus 'probability' as a prediction of future frequency.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}